@startuml Basic Proxy Pattern

' Define the styles
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam classFontStyle bold
skinparam classBackgroundColor #f8f9fa
skinparam classBorderColor #dee2e6
skinparam arrowColor #6c757d
skinparam stereotypeCBackgroundColor #e9ecef

' Title
title Basic Proxy Pattern - Core Structure

' Subject interface
interface Subject <<Subject>> {
  +request(): void
}

' RealSubject class
class RealSubject <<RealSubject>> {
  +request(): void
}

' Proxy class
class Proxy <<Proxy>> {
  -realSubject: RealSubject
  +request(): void
  -checkAccess(): boolean
  -logAccess(): void
}

' Client
class Client <<Client>> {
  -subject: Subject
  +doWork(): void
}

' Relationships
Subject <|.. RealSubject
Subject <|.. Proxy
Proxy o--> RealSubject : delegates to >
Client --> Subject : uses >

' Pattern component notes
note right of Subject
  <b>Subject (Interface)</b>

  Defines the common interface for both
  RealSubject and Proxy.

  <b>Purpose:</b>
  Allows Proxy to be used anywhere the
  RealSubject is expected. Client code
  depends on this interface, not on
  concrete implementations.

  <b>Key Characteristic:</b>
  Both Proxy and RealSubject implement
  the same interface, making them
  interchangeable from the client's
  perspective.
end note

note left of RealSubject
  <b>RealSubject</b>

  The real object that the proxy represents.
  Contains the actual business logic and
  functionality.

  <b>Characteristics:</b>
  • Implements the Subject interface
  • Performs the actual work
  • May be expensive to create/access
  • No knowledge of the proxy

  The RealSubject does its job without
  knowing it's being proxied.
end note

note right of Proxy
  <b>Proxy</b>

  Controls access to the RealSubject.
  Implements the same interface as
  RealSubject but adds additional behavior.

  <b>Responsibilities:</b>
  • Maintains reference to RealSubject
  • Implements Subject interface
  • Controls access to RealSubject
  • May perform pre/post processing
  • Delegates actual work to RealSubject

  <b>Common Proxy Operations:</b>
  • checkAccess() - verify permissions
  • logAccess() - track usage
  • lazy initialization - delay creation
  • caching - store results
  • validation - check preconditions

  <b>Delegation Pattern:</b>
  public void request() {
    if (checkAccess()) {
      realSubject.request();
      logAccess();
    }
  }
end note

note right of Client
  <b>Client</b>

  Works with Subject interface without
  knowing whether it's using a Proxy
  or RealSubject.

  <b>Usage Pattern:</b>
  Subject subject = new Proxy();
  subject.request();

  Client is decoupled from whether
  it's using the real object or a proxy.

  <b>Benefit:</b>
  Client code remains unchanged when
  proxy is introduced or removed.
end note

' Pattern flow note
note left of Proxy
  <b>Proxy Pattern Flow:</b>

  1. Client calls proxy.request()
  2. Proxy performs pre-processing (e.g., checkAccess())
  3. If access granted, Proxy creates/gets RealSubject (if needed)
  4. Proxy delegates to realSubject.request()
  5. Proxy performs post-processing (e.g., logAccess())
  6. Proxy returns result to client

  <b>Key Advantage:</b>
  Client is unaware of the proxy layer. All additional
  functionality (access control, caching, logging) is
  transparent to the client.

  <b>Lazy Initialization:</b>
  Proxy can delay creating RealSubject until first use:
  if (realSubject == null) {
    realSubject = new RealSubject();
  }
end note

' Legend
note left of Subject
  <b>Proxy Pattern Core Components:</b>

  <b>Subject:</b> Common interface for RealSubject and Proxy.
  Defines the contract that both must implement.

  <b>RealSubject:</b> The actual object doing the real work.
  Contains business logic and may be expensive to create
  or access.

  <b>Proxy:</b> Surrogate or placeholder for RealSubject.
  Controls access and may add functionality like:
  • Access control (Protection Proxy)
  • Lazy initialization (Virtual Proxy)
  • Remote access (Remote Proxy)
  • Caching (Caching Proxy)
  • Logging/monitoring (Smart Proxy)

  <b>Client:</b> Uses Subject interface. Unaware of whether
  it's interacting with Proxy or RealSubject.

  <b>Key Benefits:</b>
  • Controls access to RealSubject
  • Adds functionality without changing RealSubject
  • Can delay expensive object creation
  • Provides location transparency (Remote Proxy)
  • Enables caching and performance optimization
  • Maintains same interface as RealSubject

  <b>Intent:</b>
  Provide a surrogate or placeholder for another object
  to control access to it. Create a representative object
  that controls access to another object, which may be
  remote, expensive to create, or require protection.

  <b>Real-World Analogy:</b>
  Like a credit card that acts as a proxy for your bank
  account. The card provides controlled access to your
  funds without exposing the actual account directly.
end note

@enduml